  "parsed_answer": "Dutasteride treats hair loss by **blocking 5-alpha-reductase**, the enzyme that converts **testosterone into dihydrotestosterone (DHT)**.\n\n### In hair loss:\n- **DHT shrinks hair follicles** in people with androgenetic alopecia.\n- This leads to **shorter, thinner hairs** and eventually reduced hair growth.\n- By **lowering DHT levels**, dutasteride helps **slow or reverse follicle miniaturization** and can promote thicker hair regrowth.\n\n### Key point:\n- Dutasteride inhibits **both type I and type II 5-alpha-reductase**.\n- This makes it generally **more potent at reducing DHT** than **finasteride**, which mainly inhibits type II.\n\nIf you want, I can also explain how dutasteride compares with finasteride in terms of effectiveness and side effects.",
  "question_id": "Q132",
  "question_text": "What is the mechanism of action of Dutasteride in the context of hair-loss treatment?",
